In C “file” usually means **a stream of bytes stored outside your program’s memory**—for instance a text document, image, database record, log entry, etc.—that resides on some kind of permanent storage device (hard drive, SSD, USB stick, network share).  

C gives you two main ways to work with files:

| What it does | Typical API calls |
|--------------|-------------------|
| **Open a file** – obtain a `FILE *` descriptor so you can read/write/seek | `fopen()` / `freopen()` |
| **Close a file** – flush buffers & release resources | `fclose()` |
| **Read from a file** – sequentially pull data into your program | `getc()/getchar()`, `gets()`, `getline()`, `fread()` |
| **Write to a file** – push data out of your program | `putc()/putchar()`, `puts()`, `fprintf()`, `fwrite()` |
| **Move within a file** – change where next I/O will happen | `fseek()`, `ftell()`, `rewind()` |

### Example workflow

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* 1️⃣ Open */
    FILE *fp = fopen("example.txt","r");   // open for reading
    if (!fp) { perror("fopen failed"); return 1;}

    /* 2️⃣ Read */
    char buffer[128];
    while(fgets(buffer,sizeof buffer, fp)) {
        printf("%s",buffer);               // process line
    }

    /* 3️⃣ Close */
    fclose(fp);
}
```

#### Key points

- A `FILE *` is just a pointer to an opaque structure maintained by the standard library (`<stdio.h>`).
- File modes (“r”, “w”, “a”, “rb+”, …) tell the runtime whether you’re opening for reading/writing/appending, binary/text mode, truncating existing content, etc.
- All I/O through `<stdio.h>` is buffered unless you explicitly disable buffering with `setbuf()` or use unbuffered low‑level APIs (`_fileno()`, `_fdopen()`) or OS‐specific syscalls (`CreateFileW`, `ReadFile`, `WriteFile`).

